    {
      "id": "teach-someone",
      "pattern": "[Verb] + الـ[Direct object]",
      "exampleArabic": "أعلم الطالب",
      "exampleTransliteration": "uʿallimu ṭ-ṭāliba",
      "exampleEnglish": "I teach the student.",
      "grammarNote": "عَلَّمَ ('to teach') is the first Form II (فَعَّلَ) verb — marked by a doubled middle root letter, shown with a shadda: عَلَّمَ, not عَلَمَ. Like Form III (سافر, level 43), Form II's present-tense prefix takes damma, not fatha: أُعَلِّمُ, تُعَلِّمُ, تُعَلِّمِينَ, يُعَلِّمُ, نُعَلِّمُ — same six-pronoun template, different stem shape. Form II often carries a causative sense next to its Form I root meaning ('to know' → 'to cause to know', i.e. teach). طَالِبٌ ('a student') takes الـ exactly like any other noun.",
      "audio": { "id": "phrase_uallimu_altalib", "text": "أعلم الطالب" },
      "introducedInLevel": 54
    }
  ],
